WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.520
SPEAKER: Alerting is vital
to quickly notify you

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.530
of any problems in
your application.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.900
Cloud monitoring lets you
set up custom alerting,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.550
but it's important to set
up alerting correctly.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.310
Let's go over how you
can set up alerting,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.110
and then look at
some common mistakes

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.940
and learn how to avoid them.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.760
Alerts provide timely awareness
of operational problems

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.280
in your cloud applications, so
you can resolve the problems

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.220
quickly.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.980
For example, you may
have a system that's

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.840
having higher latency
than expected,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.460
or your production
environment might

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.620
run into a resource constraint.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.140
Here, we can see an
actual notification email

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.390
from an alert
based on CPU usage.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.070
This email shows
key information,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.890
such as what triggered
the alert, which project

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.560
it's in, and when it happened.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.337
When you create an
alerting policy,

00:00:45.337 --> 00:00:46.920
you'll need to
describe the conditions

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.420
under which you
want to be alerted

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.520
and how you want to be notified.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.280
This includes identifying
the metrics and values

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.020
that the alert will trigger on.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.330
Metrics can come from your
Google Cloud Services,

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.843
such as Compute Engine or GKE.

00:00:59.843 --> 00:01:01.260
You can also alert
on metrics that

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.709
come from your uptime
checks or SLOs.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:06.330
Notifications for
an alert can be

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.470
sent to multiple channels,
like email, SMS, or pub/sub.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.220
Adding multiple
notification channels

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.950
makes sure everyone
can be notified,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.910
no matter which
tools they're using.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:18.420
Now, let's look at how to avoid
some common mistakes teams

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.580
make when setting up alerts.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.190
The key to useful
alerts is timeliness.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.680
Some metrics may lag
behind actual usage

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.240
due to ingestion delays.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.690
This means they may not be as
useful for production alerts.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.820
For example, we can
see a few metrics here

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.710
that are sampled
every 60 seconds,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.972
but could take up to six
hours to actually report.

00:01:37.972 --> 00:01:39.930
If you want an alert on
these types of metrics,

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.555
you'd better make sure that you
can tolerate the potential wait

00:01:42.555 --> 00:01:43.080
time.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.420
Another common mistake
is using the wrong kinds

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.788
of metrics and alerts.

00:01:46.788 --> 00:01:49.080
There are three types of
metrics and five types of data

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.150
that can be used, and
it's important to know

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.860
when to use each.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.680
Types of metrics you can
use are gauge metrics,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.720
where the value represents
a specific instance in time.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.380
A common example is
measuring CPU utilization

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.120
at any point in time.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.640
Delta metrics, where the value
measures the change since it

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.842
was last recorded.

00:02:06.842 --> 00:02:08.550
One example would be
measuring the change

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.009
in requests for an API.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:14.500
And cumulative metrics, where
the value increases over time.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.160
A great example of this
is compute usage time.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.410
It's important to read the
full details on each metric

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.540
and choose the right ones
to match what you need.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.360
Not every type of metric
can use every type of value.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.250
In addition, cumulative
metrics can't

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.770
be used for alerting without
first being aggregated

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.320
into noncumulative metrics.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:32.790
It's also important
to understand

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.550
the two stages of data
aggregation, alignment

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.050
and reduction.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.020
Alignment aggregates your data
within a single time series

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:44.365
by bucketing unaligned data into
alignment periods, or windows.

00:02:44.365 --> 00:02:45.990
You can then choose
different functions

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.990
to calculate a single value
aligned to a minute boundary,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.200
such as sum, average, or max.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.630
Reduction then further
aggregates that data

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.310
by grouping the
data across labels.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.910
For example, you may align
your latency metric value

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.460
to hourly buckets using
the maximum value,

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.555
and then reduce by
regional labels.

00:03:04.555 --> 00:03:06.180
That would give you
the maximum latency

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.047
per region for each hour.

00:03:08.047 --> 00:03:09.630
Just like the types
of metrics, you'll

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.310
want to make sure that
you're aggregating data

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.185
in the way that makes
the most sense for what

00:03:13.185 --> 00:03:14.700
you're trying to measure.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.380
In addition to that,
here are two best

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.600
practices to make
sure you follow.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.550
First, the alignment
period should always

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.910
be greater than or equal to
your metrics delay time window.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.520
Otherwise, your window might
not have any data in it

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.200
due to the delay.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.470
Second, make sure you're using
multiple conditions correctly.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.720
Choosing Any means
only one condition

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.600
needs to be met for your
alert, while choosing All

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.963
means every condition
has to be met.

00:03:38.963 --> 00:03:40.380
Check out the
documentation to see

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.090
more examples and
additional details

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:43.350
about alerting and metrics.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

